---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { identity, principles, disciplines } from '../data/site';
---

<Layout title="About" description={identity.statement}>
  <div class="shell">
    <section class="opener">
      <p class="opener__kicker">About</p>
      <h1 class="display display--md">{identity.legalName}</h1>
      <p class="lede">{identity.statement}</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I started in physics because it was the only subject where being wrong was expensive. A
          model that does not match the measurement is not a matter of taste; it is finished. That
          habit has been the most useful thing I own, and I have carried it into everything since.
        </p>
        <p>
          At university I worked on the boundary between quantum and classical behaviour — the place
          where a system stops being one thing and becomes another. It is a good place to learn
          humility: the mathematics is clean, the apparatus is not, and the interesting results live
          in the gap between them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Computing came from needing an instrument that did not exist. A simulation I could not
          afford to run by hand, then a vision pipeline that had to work on hardware nobody would
          call generous, then an orchestration layer because the alternative was maintenance at
          three in the morning. I write software for the same reason I build apparatus: to find out
          whether an idea survives contact with reality.
        </p>
        <p>
          Debate is where the third register comes from. Competitive finals taught me to state an
          opposing case in its strongest form before answering it, and coaching taught me that an
          argument nobody can follow has already lost. Four years, twelve titles, and more than a
          hundred and fifty students later, it is still the fastest way I know to test whether
          something is actually true.
        </p>
        <p>
          The three are one method. Build the instrument, measure honestly, and be willing to be
          persuaded by what comes back.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section__head">
        <h2 class="title">What I work on</h2>
      </div>

      <ul class="terms">
        {disciplines.map((discipline) => <li class="terms__item">{discipline}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section__head">
        <h2 class="title">How I argue</h2>
        <p class="note">Four rules, applied to papers and code reviews as much as to debates.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="work work--plain">
        {
          principles.map((rule) => (
            <article class="work__item">
              <div class="work__row">
                <h3 class="work__name">{rule.name}</h3>
              </div>
              <p class="work__line">{rule.body}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The best conversations start with a problem rather than an introduction.
          <a class="link link--accent" href="/contact">Write to me</a> if you have one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
